I found no correctness bugs in `61ce2402..ffaa9465`. All 8 angles ran in this context, with no subagents. Every hunk keeps the bytes sent on the wire the same: field order matches the old `json!` order, which the test's own `MAIN_WIRE` expectation confirms. Empty `fileNotifications`, a `None` `flags` and the empty `getSourceFileNames` params all serialize as before. No callers outside `rpc.rs` and `declared.rs` use `Asking`. All 8 findings below are cleanup, altitude, test-coverage or convention items. I did not run any checks, builds or tests for this review.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "crates/semantic/src/checker.rs",
    "line": 492,
    "summary": "Snapshot release on the checker's drop/close path still sends a hand-built `json!({\"snapshot\": ..})` under the string \"release\", bypassing the new typed `Request`/`Release`, so the PR's claim that a method cannot be sent with another's parameters is untrue outside rpc.rs.",
    "failure_scenario": "A later change to `Release`'s parameters (or a tsrs rename of the key) updates rpc.rs and its wire test, but checker.rs:492 (and test helper on_demand.rs:843) keep sending the old shape, and tsrs reads a missing key as zero, so releases silently miss and snapshots leak with no failing test."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs",
    "line": 923,
    "summary": "The heap-budget exemption is still a string comparison `method != \"release\"` even though requests are now typed. It belongs on the `Request` trait, e.g. `fn past_heap_budget(&self) -> bool { false }` overridden by `Release`.",
    "failure_scenario": "A new memory-freeing request type, or a typo or rename of the `Release::method` literal, silently loses or keeps the exemption; checker.rs:562 repeats the same stringly check, so there are two places to keep in sync."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs",
    "line": 744,
    "summary": "`open_in_memory` now clones every root path into a `Vec<String>` where the old code borrowed `Vec<&String>`, only because `NewProgram::root_files` is typed `&[String]`.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every in-memory snapshot allocates a fresh copy of each file path. This is cheap for one probe but is wasted work on a per-request path; making `root_files` take any serializable slice (e.g. `&'a [impl AsRef<str>]` or a generic) would keep the borrow."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs",
    "line": 175,
    "summary": "`Initialize` hand-writes `impl Serialize` calling `serialize_unit()`, but `#[derive(Serialize)] struct Initialize;` already serializes a unit struct as `null` in serde_json.",
    "failure_scenario": "Eight lines of manual impl duplicate what derive gives, unlike every sibling request type, which derives."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs",
    "line": 1593,
    "summary": "The test doc comments record history (\"What `main` sent before requests were typed ... run on `main` at 61ce2402 (the `Asking` enum and `json!` parameters)\", \"Typing the requests changed no byte\"), and the test name `every_request_is_written_as_main_wrote_it` refers to a past branch state.",
    "failure_scenario": "Breaks AGENTS.md (Writing and commits): \"Comments: only the non-obvious why, no history (the commit records it).\" Once merged, the comment points at a commit and an enum that no longer exist."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs",
    "line": 1754,
    "summary": "For `updateSnapshot`, `mismatches` recurses into `CreateSnapshotParams` on `changes`, which itself checks the nested fileNotifications, createPrograms and reconfigurePrograms. The outer call then checks those same nested objects again.",
    "failure_scenario": "A single wrong key in a reconfigured program is reported twice in the assertion diff, which looks like two defects. Checking nested objects only inside the `CreateSnapshotParams` recursion, or only in the outer call, removes the duplication."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs",
    "line": 1733,
    "summary": "The key-conformance test says it checks \"the objects nested in them\", but it never checks `fileSystem` (the `{kind, files}` layer from `in_memory_layer`) or the `symbol` reference objects against what tsrs reads.",
    "failure_scenario": "A misspelled `kind` or `files` key in the in-memory layer, which tsrs ignores and reads as empty, makes in-memory probes check an empty program, and `every_key_sent_is_one_tsrs_reads` still passes."
